            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-lg",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>Your air purifier, equipped with proper filtration, can tackle a wide range of household odors. Here are the most common types:</p><h3>Pet Odors in Your Home</h3><p>Pet smells come from multiple sources, including dander, hair, saliva, urine, and general pet accidents. Over time, pet dander breaks down and releases VOCs that contribute to lingering odors.</p><p>An effective air purifier needs both particle filtration for dander and hair, plus activated carbon for the gaseous odor compounds. Running your air purifier continuously in rooms where pets spend the most time helps maintain fresher air. Explore Blueair's <a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/pet-odor-solutions-nontoxic-sustainable-air-care-that-s-safe-for-pets-and-your-home\">pet-focused air purification solutions</a> to find options that work with your household.</p><h3>Cooking Smells Eliminated by Air Purifiers</h3><p>Strong cooking odors from fish, garlic, spices, and fried foods can linger long after meal preparation ends. Cooking releases both particles (grease, smoke) and gases (aromatic compounds).</p><p>Activated carbon filters in air purifiers excel at adsorbing these food-related VOCs. For best results, position your air purifier in or near the kitchen, though not directly next to the stove, where grease could clog the filters prematurely.</p><h3>Smoke Odors and Air Purifiers</h3><p>Cigarette smoke, wildfire smoke, and other combustion odors contain both fine particles and gaseous compounds. Smoke particles are particularly small and can penetrate deep into furniture, carpets, and walls.</p><p>A combination of HEPA-type filtration and activated carbon is essential for smoke removal from your air purifier. The particle filter captures the fine smoke particles, while the carbon layer adsorbs the odor-causing gases.<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/smoke\"> Learn more about how air purifiers handle smoke</a> and discover specialized smoke-fighting filters available for Blueair purifiers.</p><h3>Chemical and Household Odors Removed by Air Purifiers</h3><p>Cleaning products, paints, new furniture, air fresheners, and personal care products all release VOCs into your home air. Some of these chemicals can linger for weeks or months after initial use.</p><p>Activated carbon filtration is particularly effective against these chemical odors because carbon naturally attracts and holds organic compounds. If you've recently painted a room or brought home new furniture, running your air purifier can significantly reduce the off-gassing smell.</p><h3>Mold and Mildew Odors in Your Home</h3><p>Musty smells from mold and mildew indicate excess moisture and biological growth. While your air purifier can remove mold spores from the air and reduce the associated odor, addressing the moisture source is critical for long-term success.</p><p>Air purifiers complement but do not replace proper mold remediation and humidity control.</p><h2>Important Limitations to Understand About Air Purifiers and Odors</h2><p>Air purifiers are powerful tools for odor control, but understanding their limitations helps set realistic expectations.</p><h3>Carbon filters have a lifespan</h3><p>Unlike HEPA filters, which can trap particles indefinitely until airflow is restricted, activated carbon fills up with adsorbed gases over time. Once saturated, carbon filters in your air purifier lose effectiveness. Most need replacement every three to six months, depending on odor levels.</p><h3>Air purifiers address airborne odors only</h3><p>Smells embedded in carpets, upholstery, curtains, and walls require cleaning or other removal methods. Your air purifier prevents odors from continuing to circulate, but cannot extract smells already absorbed into materials.</p><h3>Source control matters</h3><p>Air purifiers work best as part of a comprehensive odor management strategy. Regular cleaning, proper ventilation, addressing spills promptly, and fixing moisture problems will always be necessary. Air purifiers enhance these efforts rather than replace them.</p><h3>Room size affects air purifier performance</h3><p>Every air purifier is rated for a specific room size based on air changes per hour. Using an undersized unit in a large space reduces effectiveness. Check the manufacturer's recommendations to ensure proper coverage.</p><h2>Choosing the Right Air Purifier for Odor Removal</h2>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
