            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>When selecting an air purifier specifically for odor control, prioritize these features:</p><p><strong>Activated carbon filter</strong></p><p>Non-negotiable for odor removal. Look for substantial carbon weight, not just a thin carbon pre-filter. Some units list carbon filter weight in the specifications.</p><p><strong>Appropriate room coverage</strong></p><p>Match your air purifier's rated square footage to your room size. For strong or persistent odors, consider a unit rated for slightly larger spaces to increase the air change rate.</p><p><strong>CADR ratings</strong></p><p>Clean Air Delivery Rate indicates how quickly your air purifier can clean air. Higher CADR numbers mean faster odor removal, especially important for smoke.</p><p><strong>Filter replacement indicators</strong></p><p>Built-in alerts help you change carbon filters before they become saturated and ineffective.</p><p><strong>Quiet operation</strong></p><p>Since odor control often requires continuous operation of your air purifier, noise level matters.<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/collections/air-purifiers\"> Blueair's air purifiers</a> are engineered for efficient, quiet performance suitable for bedrooms and living spaces. It's worth noting that some air cleaning technologies beyond standard HEPA and carbon filtration such as photocatalytic oxidation (PCO) and plasma systems may generate unwanted by-products like formaldehyde or ozone as a side effect of their operation.[1] </p><p><strong>Energy efficiency</strong></p><p>Running your air purifier full-time for odor control shouldn't spike your electricity bill. Blueair units consume minimal electricity, making continuous operation practical and affordable.</p><h2>Breathe Easier with Clean Air</h2><p>Ready to tackle household odors once and for all?<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/collections/air-purifiers\"> Explore Blueair's </a>air purifiers featuring HEPASilent™ technology and activated carbon filtration. Our Swedish-engineered units deliver cleaner air quietly and efficiently, helping you and your family breathe easier at home.</p><h3>FAQs</h3><p><strong>Q. Can air purifiers remove all household odors?</strong></p><p>Air purifiers with activated carbon filters can remove most airborne odors from pets, cooking, smoke, and chemicals. However, odors absorbed into fabrics, carpets, and walls require cleaning. Your air purifier prevents circulating odors but works best combined with regular cleaning and source control.</p><p><strong>Q. How long does it take for an air purifier to remove smells?</strong></p><p>The time varies based on odor intensity, room size, and purifier strength. Mild odors may clear within an hour, while strong smells like smoke or paint fumes might take several hours or days of continuous operation. Proper room sizing is critical for faster results.</p><p><strong>Q. What's the difference between HEPA filters and activated carbon for odors in air purifiers?</strong></p><p>HEPASilent™ in your air purifier captures solid particles like dust, pollen, and pet dander, but cannot trap gaseous odor molecules. Activated carbon filters specifically target gases and VOCs through adsorption. Effective odor removal requires both filter types working together in your air purifier.</p><p><strong>Q. How often should I replace carbon filters in my air purifier?</strong></p><p>Most activated carbon filters need replacement every three to six months, depending on odor levels and usage. Heavy odor environments require more frequent changes. When you notice smells returning despite your air purifier running, the carbon filter is likely saturated.<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/collections/air-purifier-filters\"> Shop replacement filters for your air purifier</a> and consider filter subscription options for convenience.</p><p><strong>Q. Will an air purifier help with the cigarette smoke smell?</strong></p><p>Air purifiers with both HEPA-type filtration and substantial activated carbon can significantly reduce cigarette smoke odor and particles. However, smoke residue on surfaces will require cleaning. For best results, run your air purifier continuously and replace filters regularly.</p><h3>References</h3><p>[1] Ebrahimifakhar, A., Poursadegh, M., Hu, Y., Yuill, D. P., & Luo, Y. (2024). A systematic review and meta-analysis of field studies of portable air cleaners: Performance, user behavior, and by-product emissions. Science of The Total Environment, 912, 168786. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.168786</p>",
